C = [
("h1", "15. What the Passages Do Not Say"),
("p", "Claim: a position is tested as much by what it declines to say as by what it says. This chapter lists the statements commonly drawn from the two passages that the passages do not make. It does not say that every one of them is false. It says that the verses do not say them, and that a reader who holds one must hold it on other grounds, or give it up."),
("sub", "15.1 About the first man"),
("ctbl", "What the passages say of the first man, and what they leave unsaid.", [
    ("The statement", "What the text says", "What the text leaves unsaid"),
    ("Adam’s guilt is counted to every person", "Sin entered by one man, death passed upon all men, and “all have sinned” (Romans 5:12); “by one man’s disobedience many were made sinners” (Romans 5:19)", "How the one man’s act stands to each person’s sin; the sentence of 5:12 does not state it"),
    ("Sin is passed on through birth", "Sin “entered into the world,” and death “passed upon all men” (Romans 5:12)", "Any mechanism of passing. Neither passage mentions birth, nature or descent as the means"),
    ("Infants die because of Adam’s sin", "Death reigned “even over them that had not sinned after the similitude of Adam’s transgression” (Romans 5:14)", "Who the class is. The verse describes it by what it had not done, and names no one"),
    ("Everyone who dies, dies for one reason only", "Death came by sin and passed to all (Romans 5:12), and sin was in the world before the law (Romans 5:13)", "Whether Paul excludes each person’s own sin from the reason; he says “all have sinned”"),
    ("Death is natural, not a result of sin", "“by sin” (Romans 5:12)", "Nothing in the passage allows the removal of “by sin”"),
  ], [0.26, 0.38, 0.36]),
("p", "The first row deserves a note. The volume does not say that Adam’s act has no relation to anyone else’s condition. Paul says that sin and death entered and spread by one man’s act, and the volume reports it. What it declines to do is to fill in the relation with a doctrine that the sentence does not carry. Several such doctrines exist, and they are set out in the next chapter with the names of those who hold them. A reader who holds one of them is not corrected here. The reader is asked only to see where Paul’s words stop and the doctrine begins."),
("sub", "15.2 About the second man"),
("ctbl", "What the passages say of the second man, and what they leave unsaid.", [
    ("The statement", "What the text says", "What the text leaves unsaid"),
    ("Every person is justified", "“the free gift came upon all men unto justification of life” (Romans 5:18); “they which receive abundance of grace” shall reign in life (Romans 5:17)", "That every person receives. Verse 17 states the clause of reception and verse 18 does not remove it"),
    ("The gift is offered to all, and the offer is the whole meaning of “all men”", "“came upon all men” (Romans 5:18)", "The words //offered// or //available//. Paul says what came"),
    ("The second man restores what the first man lost", "“much more” (Romans 5:15, 17, 20); “that was not first which is spiritual, but that which is natural; and afterward that which is spiritual” (1 Corinthians 15:46)", "A return to the first state. The gift is “much more,” and the spiritual follows the natural"),
    ("All the dead rise together at one time", "“every man in his own order” (1 Corinthians 15:23)", "The order and company of other resurrections; the verse names two ranks, Christ and those who are Christ’s"),
    ("The risen body is not a body", "“a spiritual body” and “there is a spiritual body” (1 Corinthians 15:44)", "That “spiritual” means immaterial; the paired word “natural” does not mean made of soul"),
  ], [0.26, 0.38, 0.36]),
("sub", "15.3 About the law, the timing and the peoples"),
("ctbl", "Further statements the passages do not make.", [
    ("The statement", "What the text says", "What the text leaves unsaid"),
    ("The law is the cause of death", "“the law entered, that the offence might abound” (Romans 5:20); “the strength of sin is the law” (1 Corinthians 15:56)", "That the law causes death; Paul says in Romans 7:12 that “the law is holy, and the commandment holy, and just, and good”"),
    ("Romans 5 teaches when the resurrection occurs", "Nothing about the resurrection of the dead", "Any time, any trumpet, any kingdom"),
    ("1 Corinthians 15 teaches how sin entered the world", "“by man came death” (1 Corinthians 15:21)", "Any statement about the entrance of sin; the verse speaks of death only"),
    ("The two heads are Israel and the Body", "“all men” and “they which receive” (Romans 5); “earthy” and “heavenly” (1 Corinthians 15)", "Any mention of Israel, of a covenant, or of an assembly as the members of either head"),
    ("The two passages are the same doctrine", "Two men, one enemy, one final title", "Any reason to read the vocabulary of one into the other"),
  ], [0.26, 0.38, 0.36]),
("sub", "15.4 Why restraint is not weakness"),
("p", "A reader may ask whether so much restraint leaves the passages without force. It does not. The passages say a great deal, and the table does not subtract any of it. Romans 5 says that sin and death reached all men through one man, that the gift is greater than the harm, and that those who receive reign in life. First Corinthians 15 says that death came by a man, that the resurrection comes by a man, that the natural body is sown and the spiritual body is raised, and that those who are Christ’s will bear the heavenly image. These are large statements. They stand without the additions that have grown up around them."),
("p", "The restraint also protects the reader from a sort of argument that cuts both ways. A reader who adds a doctrine to the text in order to meet one difficulty finds that the doctrine raises another. The reader who stops at the text avoids the exchange. The cost is that some questions are left open, and the volume accepts the cost. Where Paul does not say, the volume does not say."),
]
